You needed to get that off?

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.301
- Without a doubt. Without a doubt.

00:10:57.301 --> 00:10:58.593
100%. I had to live that.

00:10:58.593 --> 00:11:00.130
I had to live that out.

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:02.460
I got to play in Dallas Stadium.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:03.340
I know what it feels like.

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:05.500
I got to be out there.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.530
- Did you grow up a football fan?

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:08.883
- 100%.

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:11.380
I grew up a Dallas Cowboys fan.

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:14.050
- Yeah, 'cause they were always on TV too.

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:15.650
- I grew up a Dallas Cowboys fan

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:21.230
but then obviously as I
matured I became a Giants fan.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:22.063
You know what I'm saying?

00:11:22.063 --> 00:11:23.290
Once I got at that point where

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:24.910
I could kinda make my own decisions.

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:27.180
'Cause my grandfather kinda
guided me to the Cowboys.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:28.570
He was real funny too.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:30.190
'Cause this is a guy
who came from the Bronx,

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:32.670
he's in Queens, but he likes the Yankees.

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:34.160
So I'm watching the Yankees

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:36.950
and everybody in my neighborhood
is like Mets fans and shit

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.340
but then, you know what I mean?

00:11:40.751 --> 00:11:42.790
They're Jets fans, and
I'm liking the Giants.

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:44.370
It was crazy. You know what I mean?

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:45.410
- The Jets and Giants,

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:48.060
neither team made the
playoffs in the seventies.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:53.060
So there's a lot of New York
people that kind of gravitated

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.410
towards the Cowboys, the
Steelers, the Raiders,

00:11:56.410 --> 00:11:57.883
during that era.
- Yup.

00:11:58.730 --> 00:11:59.890
- Which I never understand.

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:01.520
'Cause I love rooting for bad teams.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.650
'Cause I love loyalty.
I love neighborhood.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:05.230
I love eating shit.

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:07.800
But a lot of people don't want
their sports frustrating them

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:08.900
which I understand.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:12.100
Like if sports is an outlet for your life

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:14.280
who the fuck needs more headaches?

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:15.930
You know, you're trying
to escape your headaches.

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:19.110
- Absolutely right, Gary,
and I understand the loyalty,

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:22.061
I really do, but it's just hard
to be loyal to a loser, man.

00:12:22.061 --> 00:12:23.053
Like, you keep losing

00:12:23.053 --> 00:12:25.588
- Bro. That's depressing me right now.

00:12:25.588 --> 00:12:27.578
- Yeah, I'm tired of that.
- That's depressing me.

00:12:27.578 --> 00:12:29.392
So let's talk about music.

00:12:29.392 --> 00:12:31.440
When does that even hit your radar

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.333
as I'm gonna do music?

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:35.123
- When I was-

00:12:35.123 --> 00:12:39.320
I started writing when I was
around 11 or around 12, 11, 12.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:43.010
So at about 10, 11, there's
all the martial arts stuff

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:43.930
we talked about.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:45.290
And then there's the music, right?

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:48.910
And hip hop is flooding the streets.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.200
- Do you remember the
first time you understood

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:52.820
there was a different thing?

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:54.140
What was it?

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:54.973
Did they call-

00:12:54.973 --> 00:12:57.510
The first time you heard
it, was it hip hop?

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:59.670
Did they use the term rap?

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.340
What was the term in that
very, do you remember?

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:03.840
- That's an interesting question.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.910
Initially, I mean it was hip hop,

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.970
but we didn't really call it that.

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:11.470
It was more about rap music and rhyming.

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:12.960
You know what I'm saying?

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:13.793
- I remember beat boxing

00:13:13.793 --> 00:13:16.090
- That's the human beat
boxing, but it was like rap.

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:17.690
It was like rap, you know what I'm saying?

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:18.830
- Yeah, rapping.

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:21.480
- Rapping. And then rhyming as well.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:22.840
As we got more sophisticated

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:27.320
because you know the guys that I got with

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.760
we all thought about the couplets,
the content, the cadence,

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:32.710
you know, all of that.

00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:34.880
And that's when it came
more about rhyming.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:39.880
- What was the first hip
hop song that hit you hard?

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:40.753
- Well, it wasn't even-

00:13:40.753 --> 00:13:43.743
Well, it was some of the
Cold Crush Brothers routines.

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:46.900
It wasn't even on wax. The tapes.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:47.733
I was actually-

00:13:47.733 --> 00:13:50.240
Because I used to go between
Queens and North Babylon,

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:51.073
you know what I'm saying?

00:13:51.073 --> 00:13:51.906
Every year.

00:13:51.906 --> 00:13:53.870
I lived in Queens, I lived in
North Babylon, Long Island.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:55.420
Queens, North Babylon,
Queens, North Babylon.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:56.730
- Because you were going between what?

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:58.200
- [LL] My mother and my grandmother's.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.033
- Understood.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:04.150
- But there was a lot of foster kids

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:05.830
in the house next door
to me, in North Babylon.

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:07.170
- You would go back and forth,

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:08.980
or you would spend the
summer, or how did you do it?

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:11.600
- No, no, no, no. I would
literally all of the above.

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:13.120
I would go to school back and forth

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:14.510
and summers back and forth

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:17.386
and be back and forth
during the summer, and like-

00:14:17.386 --> 00:14:19.380
- Hard or easy?
- Hard.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:21.483
Like literally every other year.

00:14:22.607 --> 00:14:23.440
I think one year-
- And was that was hard?

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:26.680
And was that hard for
you or do you think you-

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:28.207
Or at the time hard, and now you're like,

00:14:28.207 --> 00:14:30.640
"Oh, that's why I can adjust to shit."

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:32.680
Was it kinda just, it's all you knew

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:34.200
and it's just, that's what it is,

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:35.840
and didn't really think twice?

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:36.673
- Well, it was...

00:14:36.673 --> 00:14:37.580
- Did you enjoy it?

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:40.499
Did you like coming back and
like it was kinda interesting?

00:14:40.499 --> 00:14:41.817
- It wasn't even that
complicated, it was just, up Todd-

00:14:41.817 --> 00:14:43.280
- [Gary] It was life.

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:44.530
- No, Todd's a pain in the ass

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:46.190
so he's gonna go to Long Island this year.

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:47.023
Up, okay. He's good, he's good.

00:14:47.023 --> 00:14:48.660
Oh, he's being a pain in the ass,

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:50.070
now let's send him back to Queens.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:52.460
Oh.
- Any time that you would move

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:55.720
into dangerous zone, they thought a reset.

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.192
- Reset. They would hit me with the reset.

00:14:58.192 --> 00:15:00.400
(both laughing)

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:01.640
- Do you think now,

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:04.883
do you think now that enabled-

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:08.300
If I said to you, what
did you gain out of that?

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:09.590
Maybe there's nothing.
Maybe there's a ton.

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:10.630
- A lot.
- I just-

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:11.720
Yeah, I believe in-

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:13.227
I think adversity-
- It schools ya.

00:15:13.227 --> 00:15:16.410
- Go ahead.
- It gave me exposure, right?

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:18.590
Because the first time
I walked in a classroom

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:20.380
in Long Island, you
know, you gotta remember

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:25.253
I'm going from a school that's
I would say 99.99% black.

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:30.450
Other than my friend Richie who's Jewish,

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:31.746
who was like my-

00:15:31.746 --> 00:15:33.950
who's like eight, nine years old,

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:35.820
who's my Jewish friend,

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:37.140
everybody's black.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.700
I got one Jewish friend
and everybody's black

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:40.854
and Richie-
- Richie had it.

00:15:40.854 --> 00:15:41.930
- He doesn't even go to my school.

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:42.980
Richie doesn't even go to my school.

00:15:42.980 --> 00:15:44.900
Richie is a family friend

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.380
because my aunt is a schoolteacher

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:49.370
and her best friend, and Lynn,

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:50.630
and so me and Richie are cool.

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:52.820
So other than Richie, it's all black.

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:54.270
But then when I go to Long Island

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:56.890
I walk in the classroom,
it's the exact opposite.

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:58.130
Or the inverse.

00:15:58.130 --> 00:15:59.670
Everybody's white.

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:01.910
And I'm like the one,
there's one black kid.

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:04.680
And I walk in and I'm the black kid.

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:05.680
And maybe there's another one.

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:09.810
And it was like, I remember my
eyes jumping out of my head,

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:13.170
but what it did was it exposed
me to different people.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:16.007
Because you know,

00:16:16.007 --> 00:16:18.490
you go to Long Island at that time,

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:20.680
you had a lot of Italians
coming from Brooklyn

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:22.000
moving to Long Island.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:22.833
You know what I'm saying?

00:16:22.833 --> 00:16:26.350
So you're meeting, so I
had like a Italian friend

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:30.250
and then you kinda find out
also just Irish friends,

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:32.470
but then you find out
about the racial dynamics

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:35.510
that I didn't know existed
when I was in Queens.

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:36.810
You know what I mean?

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:37.790
So that was.

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:40.230
- Did it come at you hard in Long Island?

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:43.400
Or did it come in medium,
or you would just say subtle

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:44.578
'cause people were cool like that?

00:16:44.578 --> 00:16:45.450
- It's subtle. It's subtle.

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:46.570
You're coming, you know, it's subtle,

00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:47.850
you know, two kids will be talking

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:48.760
and you'll walk in on them,

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:51.403
and they'll be saying something
they shouldn't be saying.

00:16:51.403 --> 00:16:52.830
They'll be saying it, you'll
catch them saying something

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:55.630
they shouldn't be saying and
then they get quiet, you know?

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:58.040
But it wasn't like...

00:16:58.040 --> 00:16:59.190
- Atrocious.

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:00.710
- No, it wasn't atrocious, man,

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:02.970
because New York is funny like that.

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:03.803
- [Gary] Yeah I agree with this.

00:17:03.803 --> 00:17:05.496
- New York is funny in the sense that,

00:17:05.496 --> 00:17:07.300
you know, and Jersey for that matter too,

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.450
in the sense that, yes there's a lot of-

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:11.447
- There's racism, but it's so-

00:17:11.447 --> 00:17:12.740
- There's also jokes.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:16.390
There's also a rivalry and a fun.

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:18.473
It's different in New
York. You know what I mean?

00:17:18.473 --> 00:17:20.215
- I know exactly what you mean. I'm 45.

00:17:20.215 --> 00:17:23.160
Like things weren't as politically correct

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:24.230
so there was-
- Exactly.

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:26.570
- I remember, people
would make racial jokes

00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:27.830
across the board.

00:17:27.830 --> 00:17:30.900
I mean, I caught a very weird time

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:35.900
because I was Russian when
the USSR was the evil enemy.

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:40.900
So I got real heat because
we were in the cold war,

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:44.613
kind of like kids after 9/11
that were from the Middle East.

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:49.640
Russia shot down a plane in third grade,

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:51.440
shot down a commercial plane,

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:53.860
and literally my class just
decided not to talk to me

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:55.280
for the week.

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:56.440
- Wow.

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:57.750
- Like we were the enemy.

00:17:57.750 --> 00:17:59.840
And so I don't talk about it a whole lot

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.560
because it's out of context now,

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:02.393
people don't understand.

00:18:02.393 --> 00:18:05.080
But like 1982, '83, '84,

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:07.530
go watch Rocky.

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:10.830
Like Russia was the enemy and people,

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:13.457
I mean, people called me spy every day.

00:18:15.288 --> 00:18:16.537
- They called you a spy?

00:18:16.537 --> 00:18:19.570
- Everyday, like, you're spying on us.

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:20.597
People-
- Hilarious.

00:18:20.597 --> 00:18:22.530
- You know what's funny,
people fought a lot.

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:23.970
I think about fighting a lot.

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:27.540
I think fighting is different now.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:28.810
I feel like it's extremes,

00:18:28.810 --> 00:18:32.560
it's either no fighting going
on in the suburbs over there,

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:34.350
or extreme shit.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:36.220
And every weekday on my Instagram,

00:18:36.220 --> 00:18:38.370
seeing something that breaks my heart.

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:39.203
But when I-

00:18:39.203 --> 00:18:41.610
I mean, I was in a lot of fights.

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:44.050
Kids were always fighting.
There was a lot of fights.

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:45.180
- I definitely grew up.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:47.430
I grew up in the knuckle game, heavy.

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:50.575
You know, that's all I did growing up.

00:18:50.575 --> 00:18:51.660
There was a lot of knuckle games.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:52.780
- You liked it?

00:18:52.780 --> 00:18:54.130
- No, I didn't like it per se.

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:57.070
But you know, when I was younger,

00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:59.410
I was slim and I had that kinda face that

00:18:59.410 --> 00:19:01.614
you wanna try to test.
- It was too pretty?

00:19:01.614 --> 00:19:03.150
- I don't know, pretty, but,

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:04.923
they just-
- It's still pretty.

00:19:04.923 --> 00:19:05.853
- I wasn't like grrr, you know,

00:19:05.853 --> 00:19:07.970
I didn't have that look, so they weren't-

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:08.803
- You didn't seem scary.

00:19:08.803 --> 00:19:10.480
So they thought they could come up on you.

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:11.880
- So I used to have to put some up,

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:13.066
put some of this on them.

00:19:13.066 --> 00:19:14.988
(fist smacking)

00:19:14.988 --> 00:19:17.830
- Do you remember what,

00:19:17.830 --> 00:19:20.520
let's call it from six to 16,

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:23.020
the best feeling in the fight, and the-

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:24.414
Let's talk about stories
that people don't know.

00:19:24.414 --> 00:19:25.830
Like, I'll give you mine.

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:28.740
In second grade, there was
a kid named Odette Weinstock

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:32.610
who came from Israel and just
decided that we were rivals.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:36.470
We fought three times between
second and sixth grade

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:38.510
and it culminated after school

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:40.720
with literally a hundred people watching,

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:42.970
'cause he caught me earlier in the day,

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:46.800
pushed me into the locker,
and got on top of me.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:49.210
And that was it, they broke
it up, we went to detention.

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:51.500
But at lunch, I just
hear the whole lunch room

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:53.860
in sixth grade, saying,
Odette beat up Gary,

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.070
and I was like, man,
I can't, this I can't.

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:59.100
This can't be my reputation.
We just got to junior high.

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:00.800
And I walked up to him,

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:03.267
literally like fucking eighties
movies shit, and was like,

00:20:03.267 --> 00:20:05.400
"Let's fight at Marcos's house."

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:06.592
'Cause that was the hill.

00:20:06.592 --> 00:20:08.010
(LL laughing)

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:09.560
Big shout out to Marcos,
if you're watching,

00:20:09.560 --> 00:20:10.720
please email me.

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:13.260
Marcos's house, where
the ice cream truck was,

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:15.020
and literally there
was hundreds of people.

00:20:15.020 --> 00:20:17.500
We fought and he was actually winning,

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:20.420
and I would argue that I got lucky slash

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:22.120
I just refused to go down like that.

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:24.880
He was on top of me, kind
of ready to win the fight

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:27.350
and I kept rocking and I got him off,

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:29.060
and as we were both on the ground,

00:20:29.060 --> 00:20:32.040
I had the leverage and I
kicked him so hard in the face,

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:34.830
his face exploded, bleeding everywhere.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:36.870
We kinda both stood up off the kick

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:38.920
and he just started crying and ran away.

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:40.210
And for the next 48 hours

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:41.604
was the greatest moment of my life.

00:20:41.604 --> 00:20:44.104
(LL laughing)

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:47.440
But earlier that morning was probably the-

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:50.060
'cause of that micro,
I'll call it a micro loss,

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:51.110
'cause it's some bullshit,

00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:52.070
we didn't even get into it,

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:55.000
