00:46:39.630 --> 00:46:43.590
I think the way they represented
Queens was unbelievable.

00:46:43.590 --> 00:46:44.870
The way they do represent Queens.

00:46:44.870 --> 00:46:46.830
We did a song on my "G.O.A.T" album

00:46:46.830 --> 00:46:49.611
called "Killer Queens,"
You know what I'm saying?

00:46:49.611 --> 00:46:51.390
♪ And Queens got the vets ♪

00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:52.223
It was dope.

00:46:52.223 --> 00:46:53.683
And, yo,

00:46:54.550 --> 00:46:56.110
the way he wrote,

00:46:56.110 --> 00:46:58.620
the way he would make things
rhyme and then not rhyme,

00:46:58.620 --> 00:47:02.328
the way he would play with
language, play with words.

00:47:02.328 --> 00:47:04.680
♪ Queens, get the money
long time, low cash ♪

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:05.580
You know what I mean?

00:47:05.580 --> 00:47:07.760
I mean, he was just...

00:47:07.760 --> 00:47:09.730
- I remember I was
like, this guy's clever.

00:47:09.730 --> 00:47:11.400
I remember using that word.

00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:16.300
- Clever, but he had a way of
sounding authentically tough.

00:47:16.300 --> 00:47:19.140
That's not easy to come across.

00:47:19.140 --> 00:47:22.400
You could sound angry on a
record, that's one thing.

00:47:22.400 --> 00:47:23.780
You could sound angry.

00:47:23.780 --> 00:47:26.140
You could sound aggressive.

00:47:26.140 --> 00:47:29.210
But to be able to talk

00:47:29.210 --> 00:47:32.730
to be calm and really
sound authentically tough,

00:47:32.730 --> 00:47:35.026
the only two people to me
that really sound like that

00:47:35.026 --> 00:47:37.708
are Prodigy and Rakim.

00:47:37.708 --> 00:47:39.430
Where the person is not

00:47:39.430 --> 00:47:42.000
they're not yelling, but they
sound aggressive and tough.

00:47:42.000 --> 00:47:43.035
- Let's keep this Rock the Bells themed

00:47:43.035 --> 00:47:44.080
- [LL] You know what I mean?

00:47:44.080 --> 00:47:45.670
- Little fun game, I'm
enjoying this a little bit

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:46.503
for my own self.

00:47:46.503 --> 00:47:47.700
What about EPMD?

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:49.340
These are things that I just think

00:47:49.340 --> 00:47:51.108
the youngsters don't know.
- Yeah.

00:47:51.108 --> 00:47:53.290
- And are all time classic talk,

00:47:53.290 --> 00:47:55.270
we're talking all time
classic talk right now,

00:47:55.270 --> 00:47:58.874
but I would argue underrated,
which is why I love this.

00:47:58.874 --> 00:47:59.854
- Without a doubt.

00:47:59.854 --> 00:48:04.854
EPMD is a group that the
kids should check out.

00:48:04.970 --> 00:48:06.700
You know, at first,
when they first came out

00:48:06.700 --> 00:48:09.210
people used to tease 'em and
say they sounded like Rakim.

00:48:09.210 --> 00:48:10.960
Sounded like Eric B and Rakim.

00:48:10.960 --> 00:48:15.960
But yo, the beat selection
that Eric Sermon would make,

00:48:16.670 --> 00:48:19.260
me and Parish would always
get into conversations

00:48:19.260 --> 00:48:21.040
about money and real estate and shit,

00:48:21.040 --> 00:48:22.500
and be sitting around
talking about real estate.

00:48:22.500 --> 00:48:25.450
We did a record together called "Rampage."

00:48:25.450 --> 00:48:27.110
It's a record called "Rampage."

00:48:27.110 --> 00:48:29.730
The kids should check out EPMD, "Rampage"

00:48:29.730 --> 00:48:30.563
featuring LL Cool J.

00:48:30.563 --> 00:48:33.363
It's one of, both of
ours, our best classics.

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:35.700
It's dope.

00:48:35.700 --> 00:48:37.760
And they just, they came from Long Island.

00:48:37.760 --> 00:48:39.010
EPMD is from Long Island.

00:48:39.010 --> 00:48:41.780
So you know, and Long Island,

00:48:41.780 --> 00:48:43.860
you know and Rakim is
from Long Island as well.

00:48:43.860 --> 00:48:44.870
He's from Wyandanch,

00:48:44.870 --> 00:48:47.021
which is right down the
block from North Babylon,

00:48:47.021 --> 00:48:49.250
you can literally walk
down his neighborhood

00:48:49.250 --> 00:48:50.620
from where I lived in North Babylon.

00:48:50.620 --> 00:48:51.898
So it's like-

00:48:51.898 --> 00:48:52.731
- A connection.

00:48:52.731 --> 00:48:54.260
- It was a vibe. It was a vibe.

00:48:54.260 --> 00:48:56.770
EPMD just was so official. So official.

00:48:56.770 --> 00:49:00.040
- What about something
that was like a hot minute

00:49:00.040 --> 00:49:02.400
that just had a fever pitch to it.

00:49:02.400 --> 00:49:03.480
I'd love to get your perspective.

00:49:03.480 --> 00:49:05.190
You were in a different part
of your career at this point.

00:49:05.190 --> 00:49:07.230
What about Foxy Brown's moment?

00:49:07.230 --> 00:49:08.980
- Well, I launched Foxy Brown.

00:49:08.980 --> 00:49:11.690
Like, you know, the "I Shot Ya" record

00:49:11.690 --> 00:49:14.420
we put Fox, well Chris
and the Trackmasters

00:49:14.420 --> 00:49:16.197
put Foxy on the record,

00:49:16.197 --> 00:49:18.350
and when I heard it, I didn't object.

00:49:18.350 --> 00:49:20.530
I was like, "Yeah, let
her stay. Let us stay."

00:49:20.530 --> 00:49:21.860
They didn't do it. They didn't ask.

00:49:21.860 --> 00:49:23.440
They just said, "Yo, listen to this."

00:49:23.440 --> 00:49:26.470
And I heard it, and I said,
"Go ahead, let her rock."

00:49:26.470 --> 00:49:27.580
And she...

00:49:27.580 --> 00:49:29.490
- She was fire!
- Solid. Solid.

00:49:29.490 --> 00:49:32.840
- That year it just was
like fever pitch with her.

00:49:32.840 --> 00:49:36.180
- Solid. Definitely solid,
definitely dope voice.

00:49:36.180 --> 00:49:40.770
Definitely like, you know,
tomboyish, but feminine,

00:49:40.770 --> 00:49:42.010
it was like she had a good-

00:49:42.010 --> 00:49:43.360
She was magic.

00:49:43.360 --> 00:49:45.623
You know, she is a very talented act.

00:49:46.520 --> 00:49:48.530
You know what I'm saying?

00:49:48.530 --> 00:49:50.370
But there's a lot of
talented acts out there now.

00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:52.230
Like you think about a Kendrick

00:49:52.230 --> 00:49:54.976
and you think about the
way he plays with rhythms,

00:49:54.976 --> 00:49:56.160
with his voice.

00:49:56.160 --> 00:49:58.580
Even somebody, may he rest
in peace, like Nipsey,

00:49:58.580 --> 00:50:00.210
you know, so intelligent.

00:50:00.210 --> 00:50:01.043
You know what I'm saying?

00:50:01.043 --> 00:50:02.310
So smart. So brilliant.

00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:03.550
I know you were close with him.

00:50:03.550 --> 00:50:05.800
I saw different times you spoke with him.

00:50:05.800 --> 00:50:08.500
I mean, so that's kinda
like that mid generation.

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:11.340
He was very, very special.

00:50:11.340 --> 00:50:14.150
What Da Baby's doing now
is very, very clever.

00:50:14.150 --> 00:50:14.983
You know the way-

00:50:14.983 --> 00:50:16.570
- He just understands the world.

00:50:16.570 --> 00:50:18.313
Talk to me about, not to cut off

00:50:18.313 --> 00:50:21.580
because I can talk about
Da Baby for a hot minute,

00:50:21.580 --> 00:50:24.595
but talk to me about, I like
what you said about MC Lyte,

00:50:24.595 --> 00:50:27.500
and I don't wanna run out
of time without asking you,

00:50:27.500 --> 00:50:29.872
on that same kick,

00:50:29.872 --> 00:50:33.830
what was the track, the
record that you heard

00:50:34.690 --> 00:50:39.020
that literally was the
biggest quantum jump for you.

00:50:39.020 --> 00:50:41.850
Like from your, you know,
one man's point of view,

00:50:41.850 --> 00:50:43.400
you heard it and you were like,

00:50:44.361 --> 00:50:45.827
"Oh my God."

00:50:46.690 --> 00:50:47.933
Did anything stop you in your track?

00:50:47.933 --> 00:50:51.003
- No, no. Yeah. "My Uzi
Weighs a Ton" by Public Enemy.

00:50:52.367 --> 00:50:54.440
"My Uzi Weighs a Ton" by Public Enemy.

00:50:54.440 --> 00:50:57.840
- Do you remember actually.
- 100% I remember.

00:50:57.840 --> 00:50:58.680
I remember shit.

00:50:58.680 --> 00:51:02.870
I'm in the parking lot of
White Castle with a Jeep,

00:51:02.870 --> 00:51:04.413
with the seat leaned back, with my man.

00:51:04.413 --> 00:51:06.610
And that shit came on the fucking,

00:51:06.610 --> 00:51:08.540
one of the mixes on
the radio or something.

00:51:08.540 --> 00:51:10.447
And that shit, he said, you know,

00:51:11.459 --> 00:51:14.009
(imitates hip hop beats)

00:51:14.009 --> 00:51:14.842
You know what I mean?

00:51:14.842 --> 00:51:16.574
♪ My uzi weighs a ton, hold it now ♪

00:51:16.574 --> 00:51:18.152
(imitates hip hop beats)

00:51:18.152 --> 00:51:19.990
♪ Get up, get down. ♪

00:51:19.990 --> 00:51:20.823
That shit was like,

00:51:20.823 --> 00:51:22.335
♪ My uzi weighs a ton ♪

00:51:22.335 --> 00:51:23.320
(Gary laughing)

00:51:23.320 --> 00:51:24.950
I mean, that shit was like,

00:51:24.950 --> 00:51:27.210
yo me and my man was like, what the fuck?

00:51:27.210 --> 00:51:29.630
And here's the funny shit.

00:51:29.630 --> 00:51:32.067
Rick Rubin had just asked
me a few months later,

00:51:32.067 --> 00:51:33.860
"Yo I got this group, Public Enemy,

00:51:33.860 --> 00:51:35.070
you know, I'm thinking about putting out.

00:51:35.070 --> 00:51:37.140
If I could buy a song from them

00:51:37.140 --> 00:51:38.223
would you wanna perform it?"

00:51:38.223 --> 00:51:40.867
Then I was like, "I write my own shit."

00:51:42.151 --> 00:51:43.490
And I still stand by that

00:51:43.490 --> 00:51:44.802
but I kinda should've took that shit.

00:51:44.802 --> 00:51:46.427
(both laughing)

00:51:46.427 --> 00:51:49.350
That motherfucking record was so cold.

00:51:49.350 --> 00:51:52.540
Yo, when I heard "My Uzi Weighs a Ton,"

00:51:52.540 --> 00:51:54.260
that shit was like shape-shifting.

00:51:54.260 --> 00:51:56.830
I haven't felt that yet.

00:51:56.830 --> 00:51:58.230
Like that shit was like,

00:51:58.230 --> 00:52:01.940
'cause the way the Bomb Squad,
Hank Shockley and Eric Sadler

00:52:01.940 --> 00:52:03.510
the way him, Chuck and all of 'em,

00:52:03.510 --> 00:52:04.773
the way they worked with that

00:52:04.773 --> 00:52:06.950
and the way they played with technology

00:52:06.950 --> 00:52:09.780
because that was the
first time that somebody

00:52:09.780 --> 00:52:13.310
really took advantage of the technology,

00:52:13.310 --> 00:52:14.640
well, took it to another level.

00:52:14.640 --> 00:52:17.140
Marley did it in a different
way with the sampling

00:52:17.140 --> 00:52:19.823
but they took it to a whole other level.

00:52:21.060 --> 00:52:22.270
Marley kinda

00:52:22.270 --> 00:52:25.530
when he was doing some of
those Biz Markie records

00:52:25.530 --> 00:52:26.610
those were moments too,

00:52:26.610 --> 00:52:28.280
like, "Make the Music with Your Mouth"

00:52:28.280 --> 00:52:31.060
and "Rhyming with the-"

00:52:31.060 --> 00:52:32.250
Well, "Make the Music with Your Mouth"

00:52:32.250 --> 00:52:33.090
and the other record,

00:52:33.090 --> 00:52:35.070
yes, y'all, "Nobody Beats the Biz."

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:36.930
Those were kinda like quantum,

00:52:36.930 --> 00:52:40.433
they moved the needle
like maybe three points up

00:52:42.530 --> 00:52:44.870
but fuck it, Public Enemy was like 100.

00:52:44.870 --> 00:52:46.760
You know, that shit was like...

00:52:46.760 --> 00:52:47.890
It just...

00:52:47.890 --> 00:52:48.930
It was outta control.

00:52:48.930 --> 00:52:50.780
And I think that...

00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:52.090
